# netsurf: <img> load / error events, complete / naturalWidth, inline
# onload / onerror, <body onload>, new Image() as a real element (patch 250)
# Generated 2026-10-11
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 249)
#
# No <img> ever fired load or error; complete, naturalWidth and
# naturalHeight did not exist (undefined); width was the attribute string;
# inline onload= / onerror= attributes were never compiled (only 14 UI
# events were), so <body onload> did nothing either; and new Image() was a
# stand-in object whose src was a plain property -- it never fetched or
# fired. Scripts that wait for an image (lightboxes, masonry grids,
# carousels, imagesLoaded, `img.onload = ...; img.src = url` preloaders)
# waited forever. Chromium fires all of these.
#
#  - html/object.c: a per-page table of each <img>'s last image (URL,
#    state, natural size). An object fetch that settles for an <img>
#    records it and queues load / error; so does the adoption of an
#    already-decoded speculative fetch by its box (the preload scanner
#    fetched it before the box existed), and an object that settles inside
#    box construction (a cached image, before the box has its element) is
#    resolved once the box has one -- polled only while the boxes are
#    still being built (a generated-content box never gets an element). Events fire from a scheduled callback,
#    never inside the cache callback, once per element and URL (a box-tree
#    rebuild re-adopts objects and reports DONE again). An <img> outside
#    the document gets a fetch of its own (html_image_fetch_detached; 32 in
#    flight per page at most), released when it settles -- the cache keeps
#    the data for the <img> that usually follows a preload. A superseded
#    or finished fetch's handle is released (never aborted: aborting a
#    handle whose content another user shares clones the content, and
#    during that content's DONE broadcast the clone asserted in
#    content_set_ready -- 1 github.com run in 6 before this was found)
#    from the scheduled callback, not inside a cache callback. Freed with
#    the content.
#  - html/html.c: the queued image events fire before window load, as in
#    browsers; the table is freed in html_destroy.
#  - quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c: __vitaImgState(el) / __vitaImgFetch(el, src);
#    onload / onerror join the inline handler attributes; on <body> /
#    <frameset> they set window.onload / onerror (unless a script did).
#    A "load" event no longer reaches window listeners through the
#    document fan-out (DOM "get the parent": a Document's parent is its
#    Window unless the event is load). With element load events now
#    common, web-vitals' TTFB helper -- a capturing window load listener
#    that re-adds itself while readyState is not "complete" -- doubled with
#    every image event until bbc.com ran out of heap (4 runs in 6; 5111
#    window listeners); with the rule, 0 in 6, 25-31 listeners.
#  - vita_polyfill.js: complete, naturalWidth, naturalHeight, decode(), and
#    width / height (rendered, else the attribute, else natural) for <img>;
#    src answers the absolute URL; setting src (or setAttribute('src')) on
#    an <img> outside the document fetches it; new Image(w, h) is an <img>
#    element (instanceof Image and HTMLImageElement).
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-image-load.html IMGLOAD 11/11 (Chromium 11/11;
# check 11, element loads kept off a capturing window listener, fails
# with the fan-out rule disarmed).
# Before this patch a probe of the same cases saw no load / error at all,
# complete / naturalWidth undefined, and new Image() never fetching.
# Known difference: a listener added by script runs before a parse-time
# onload= attribute (dispatchEvent calls listeners before the on* slot;
# browsers order by registration).
#
